## Defect found — criterion 3, budget_fit.go note is NOT honest about a clamped/ended entry
Criterion 3 explicitly extends the "must not invent the lost month" rule to
`budget_fit.go`: "`budget_fit.go` notes 'starts <Mon YYYY>' / 'through <Mon
YYYY>' derived through the same function." Ruling 2026-09-16e's own wording
("the rollover clamps a past start to 0 and a past end to 0, losing the
original months, so the row must not invent them") is figure-agnostic — it
is about the ENTRY's classification, not about one specific template.

`internal/services/retirement/analysis/budget_fit.go` (touched by RC3
attempt 1, 1f9a35a, untouched by attempt 2 — `git diff 1f9a35a a57394c --
internal/services/retirement/analysis/budget_fit.go` is empty) still builds
the expense-breakdown note unconditionally from `source.EndMonth`:

    if source.EndMonth != nil {
        note = scheduleNote("through", s.StartDate, *source.EndMonth-1)
    }

For a clamped/ended entry (EndMonth == 0, the only value `scheduleEnded`
tests for), this computes `scheduleNote("through", StartDate, -1)` — i.e.
"through <the month immediately before the plan start>". That is not the
entry's real end month (the clamp discarded that), it is an artifact of the
`-1` offset arithmetic on a sentinel value. This is the EXACT fabrication
pattern ruling 2026-09-16e forbade in the templates for this same entry
class, just in a different surface.

Reproduced twice:
1. Analysis-package unit probe (`TestProbeBudgetFitEndedExpenseNoteIsHonest`,
   internal/services/retirement/analysis/rc3_adversarial_probe_test.go):
   built a WhatIfSettings with StartDate "2026-10" and one ExpenseSource
   {StartMonth:0, EndMonth:&0}, called `BudgetFit`, read the returned note:
       note for ended item OldLease: "through Sep 2026"
   FAILED the assertion that an ended/clamped note must say "ended" rather
   than fabricate a specific through-month.
2. Full page render, same clamp mechanics used by the shipped oracle
   (StartDate 20 months old, real rollover applied via `resolveCurrentMonth`,
   `TestProbeBudgetFitPageShowsFabricatedThroughNote`,
   internal/handlers/whatif/rc3_adversarial_probe2_test.go): GET /whatif on
   a plan holding a clamped/ended expense "PageLease" (StartMonth 0, EndMonth
   0 after the real 20-month shift) renders it correctly as "Ended before the
   plan start" in the expense-sources-list card (attempt-2's fix, working as
   intended) AND, twice more in the SAME response body — the Budget Fit /
   cash-flow-breakdown card (`web/templates/components/whatif/budget-analysis.html:31,352`,
   both the main render and its OOB-mirrored copy) — as:

       PageLease <span class="text-xs italic">(through Aug 2026)</span>
       $0.00/mo

   "Aug 2026" is the month before the (20-months-shifted) plan start, not
   the entry's real, discarded end month. The $0.00/mo and opacity-40
   styling correctly signal "not counted", but the parenthetical still
   states a specific, fabricated calendar month as fact next to the item's
   name — the same entry the schedule card, three lines away in the same
   response, correctly describes as having ended before the plan start with
   the month unknown.

This is a split-classification defect in the terms CLAUDE.md names directly:
one surface (expense/income-sources-list.html, via `scheduleEnded`) was
updated for the clamped/ended entry class; a second surface rendering the
SAME entry's schedule status (budget_fit.go's expense-breakdown note, wired
to budget-analysis.html) was not enumerated and still fabricates a month.
Both are within RC3's own introduced code (both the ExpenseSource
month-offset model and `scheduleNote` were introduced by RC3 attempt 1,
1f9a35a) and criterion 3 names budget_fit.go by name as in scope.

Remedy the fix would need: guard the expense-breakdown loop with
`scheduleEnded(source.EndMonth)` (or equivalent) and substitute an honest
note ("ended before the plan start" / omit the note entirely) instead of
`scheduleNote("through", …, -1)| for that case — mirroring the guard already
applied in the two list templates. That remedy is a small, in-scope change
to a file the task's own criterion 3 names; it is not a pre-existing,
unrelated defect.

